mailbox.resource/--background--               mailbox.resource/--background--

   VERSION MARKS
	The calls of this resource are described one by one below. This entry
	says since when each exists, and how a change is told, as the autodocs
	of the system do (see exec.library/AllocPooled or StackSwap).

	A call that was added after the first version of the resource has its
	version at the end of the line of its NAME, like "(V1.2)": the number is
	the version of the resource in which it appeared. A change in what a call
	does is written in its FUNCTION or NOTE text, "As of V1.1 ..." and "Prior
	to V1.1 ...". A call without a version in its NAME has been there since
	1.0.

   VERSIONS
	The history comes from the repository of the project and from the one
	that it was taken out of (Emu68-tools-old).

	1.0   2025-10-13  First version: MB_RawCommand, MB_StringCommand,
	                  MB_GetClockRate, MB_SetClockRate, MB_GetClockState,
	                  MB_SetClockState, MB_GetPowerState, MB_SetPowerState,
	                  MB_GetGPIOState and MB_SetGPIOState.
	1.1   2025-11-08  Every call gives up after three seconds without an
	                  answer from the firmware and reports it (see the
	                  calls). No call added.
	1.2   2026-10-17  MB_ObtainLock and MB_ReleaseLock added. No other call
	                  changed.

   VERSION AND REVISION
	lib_Version and lib_Revision of the resource. In 1.1 they were left as
	in 1.0, written by hand: lib_Revision stayed at 0 while the id string
	said 1.1. So a resource of 1.0 and one of 1.1 both have lib_Revision 0.
	From 1.2 the id string, lib_Version and lib_Revision all come from the
	version of the project (1.2 gives lib_Version 1 and lib_Revision 2).

	A program that needs the lock calls (V1.2) tests lib_Revision >= 2. It
	cannot tell 1.0 from 1.1 by lib_Revision, and nothing depends on it:
	no call exists in one and not in the other.

   SEE ALSO
	exec.library/OpenResource()

mailbox.resource/MB_GetClockRate             mailbox.resource/MB_GetClockRate

   NAME
	MB_GetClockRate -- read a clock's configured rate

   SYNOPSIS
	rate = MB_GetClockRate( clock_id )
	d0                      d0

	ULONG MB_GetClockRate(ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Reads the configured rate of a VideoCore-managed clock, using
	property tag GET_CLOCK_RATE (0x00030002).

   INPUTS
	clock_id        The clock to query, by the firmware's id: 1 EMMC, 2 UART,
	                3 ARM, 4 CORE, 5 V3D (the firmware documents the others).

   RESULTS
	rate            The clock's configured rate in Hz, or -1 on a mailbox
	                transport error.

   EXAMPLES
	#include <proto/exec.h>
	#include <proto/dos.h>
	#include <proto/mailbox.h>

	APTR MailboxBase = OpenResource("mailbox.resource");
	if (MailboxBase)
	{
	    ULONG rate = MB_GetClockRate(3);                   /* 3 = ARM clock */
	    Printf("ARM clock: %ld Hz\n", rate);
	}

   NOTE
	As of V1.1 the call gives up after three seconds without an answer from
	the firmware and returns -1. Prior to V1.1 it had no time limit and no
	way to report a failure: a mailbox that did not answer stopped the task
	for good.

   SEE ALSO
	mailbox.resource/MB_SetClockRate()

mailbox.resource/MB_GetClockState           mailbox.resource/MB_GetClockState

   NAME
	MB_GetClockState -- read a clock's enabled/disabled state

   SYNOPSIS
	state = MB_GetClockState( clock_id )
	d0                        d0

	ULONG MB_GetClockState(ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Reads whether a VideoCore-managed clock is currently enabled,
	using property tag GET_CLOCK_STATE (0x00030001).

   INPUTS
	clock_id        The clock to query, by the firmware's id: 1 EMMC, 2 UART,
	                3 ARM, 4 CORE, 5 V3D.

   RESULTS
	state           Bit 0 set if the clock exists and is on, or -1 on a
	                mailbox transport error.

   EXAMPLES
	#include <proto/exec.h>
	#include <proto/dos.h>
	#include <proto/mailbox.h>

	APTR MailboxBase = OpenResource("mailbox.resource");
	if (MailboxBase)
	{
	    ULONG state = MB_GetClockState(3);                  /* 3 = ARM clock */
	    Printf("ARM clock state: %ld\n", state);
	}

   NOTE
	As of V1.1 the call gives up after three seconds without an answer from
	the firmware and returns -1. Prior to V1.1 it had no time limit and no
	way to report a failure: a mailbox that did not answer stopped the task
	for good.

   SEE ALSO
	mailbox.resource/MB_SetClockState()

mailbox.resource/MB_GetGPIOState             mailbox.resource/MB_GetGPIOState

   NAME
	MB_GetGPIOState -- read a GPIO/LED expander pin state

   SYNOPSIS
	state = MB_GetGPIOState( gpio )
	d0                       d0

	ULONG MB_GetGPIOState(ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Reads the state of an expander GPIO/LED pin, using property tag
	GET_LED_STATUS (0x00030041), documented under this name because
	the same tag is what the mailbox.resource author uses for
	general GPIO pin access.

   INPUTS
	gpio            The GPIO number, offset by 128 internally before being
	                sent to the firmware: 42 is the status LED, 130 the power
	                LED.

   RESULTS
	state           The pin's state, or -1 on a mailbox transport error.

   EXAMPLES
	#include <proto/exec.h>
	#include <proto/dos.h>
	#include <proto/mailbox.h>

	APTR MailboxBase = OpenResource("mailbox.resource");
	if (MailboxBase)
	{
	    ULONG state = MB_GetGPIOState(42);                  /* 42 = status LED */
	    Printf("Status LED: %ld\n", state);
	}

   NOTE
	As of V1.1 the call gives up after three seconds without an answer from
	the firmware and returns -1. Prior to V1.1 it had no time limit and no
	way to report a failure: a mailbox that did not answer stopped the task
	for good.

   SEE ALSO
	mailbox.resource/MB_SetGPIOState()

mailbox.resource/MB_GetPowerState           mailbox.resource/MB_GetPowerState

   NAME
	MB_GetPowerState -- read a device's power on/off state

   SYNOPSIS
	state = MB_GetPowerState( id )
	d0                        d0

	ULONG MB_GetPowerState(ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Reads whether a VideoCore-managed device domain is powered on,
	using property tag GET_POWER_STATE (0x00020001).

   INPUTS
	id              The device to query, by the firmware's id: 0 SD card,
	                3 USB host controller (the firmware documents the others).

   RESULTS
	state           Bit 0 set if the device is on, or -1 on a mailbox
	                transport error.

   EXAMPLES
	#include <proto/exec.h>
	#include <proto/dos.h>
	#include <proto/mailbox.h>

	APTR MailboxBase = OpenResource("mailbox.resource");
	if (MailboxBase)
	{
	    ULONG state = MB_GetPowerState(0);                  /* 0 = SD card */
	    Printf("SD card power: %ld\n", state);
	}

   NOTE
	As of V1.1 the call gives up after three seconds without an answer from
	the firmware and returns -1. Prior to V1.1 it had no time limit and no
	way to report a failure: a mailbox that did not answer stopped the task
	for good.

   SEE ALSO
	mailbox.resource/MB_SetPowerState()

mailbox.resource/MB_ObtainLock                 mailbox.resource/MB_ObtainLock

   NAME
	MB_ObtainLock -- keep the mailbox for a sequence of commands (V1.2)

   SYNOPSIS
	MB_ObtainLock()

	VOID MB_ObtainLock(VOID);

   FUNCTION
	Takes the lock that serialises the commands, and keeps it until
	MB_ReleaseLock().

	Every command is serialised on its own, but a sequence of
	commands is not: another task can send a message in between.
	That matters when the firmware keeps a state from one message to
	the next, for example a selection made by the first message and
	used by the next. A task which holds the lock sees no other
	command until it releases it, so the sequence cannot be cut.

	The lock is a SignalSemaphore. It nests for the task which owns
	it, so the calls made between MB_ObtainLock() and
	MB_ReleaseLock() simply take it again. Each MB_ObtainLock()
	needs its MB_ReleaseLock().

   EXAMPLES
	#include <proto/exec.h>
	#include <proto/dos.h>
	#include <proto/mailbox.h>

	APTR MailboxBase = OpenResource("mailbox.resource");
	if (MailboxBase)
	{
	    ULONG select[7] = { 7 * 4, 0, 0x00048013, 4, 0, 1, 0 };
	    ULONG pitch[7]  = { 7 * 4, 0, 0x00040008, 4, 0, 0, 0 };

	    MB_ObtainLock();
	    MB_RawCommand(select);      /* SET_DISPLAY_NUM 1 */
	    MB_RawCommand(pitch);       /* GET_PITCH, of that display */
	    MB_ReleaseLock();

	    Printf("Pitch: %ld\n", pitch[5]);
	}

   NOTE
	The other tasks wait while the lock is held: take it, send the
	commands, release it. Do not call it from an interrupt, and do
	not wait for anything else while holding it. A task which ends
	without releasing it leaves the mailbox locked for everyone.

	The call exists from version 1.2 of the resource. On an older one
	the vector is not there and the call would jump into nothing:
	check lib_Version and lib_Revision of the resource first.

   SEE ALSO
	mailbox.resource/MB_ReleaseLock(),
	mailbox.resource/MB_RawCommand()

mailbox.resource/MB_RawCommand                 mailbox.resource/MB_RawCommand

   NAME
	MB_RawCommand -- send a raw VideoCore property-tag buffer

   SYNOPSIS
	MB_RawCommand( command )
	               a0

	VOID MB_RawCommand(ULONG *);

   FUNCTION
	MB_RawCommand sends a caller-built VideoCore mailbox property-
	tag buffer as-is, over the property channel (mailbox channel 8),
	and waits for the firmware's reply. It handles the semaphore
	locking, endianness byte-swapping and cache DMA flushing
	internally, then swaps the buffer contents back to big-endian
	before returning, so the caller's buffer is portable m68k data
	on both sides of the call.

	This is the escape hatch for any property tag that does not
	(yet) have a dedicated function in this resource. A message may
	carry several tags one after the other: they are sent together,
	in one round trip, and no other task comes in between them. Several
	messages that must not be separated are kept together with
	MB_ObtainLock().

   INPUTS
	command         A pointer to a property-tag buffer, big-endian, built
	                exactly as documented on the raspberrypi/firmware
	                wiki's Mailbox-property-interface page. command[0]
	                must hold the buffer's total size in bytes. The buffer
	                may not exceed 480 longwords.

   RESULTS
	None. On success, the buffer is overwritten in place with the
	firmware's reply (already byte-swapped back to big-endian). On
	mailbox failure or timeout, command[0] is set to 0xffffffff.

   EXAMPLES
	#include <proto/exec.h>
	#include <proto/dos.h>
	#include <proto/mailbox.h>

	APTR MailboxBase = OpenResource("mailbox.resource");
	if (MailboxBase)
	{
	    ULONG command[7] = { 7 * 4, 0, 0x00000001, 4, 0, 0, 0 };

	    MB_RawCommand(command);
	    Printf("Firmware revision: %ld\n", command[5]);
	}

   NOTE
	This call is not reentrant with itself or with any other
	function of this resource while it is running: it is serialised
	internally by the resource's own semaphore, so concurrent
	callers simply queue up.

	As of V1.1 the call gives up after three seconds without an answer from
	the firmware and sets command[0] to 0xffffffff. Prior to V1.1 it had
	no time limit and no way to report a failure: a mailbox that did not
	answer stopped the task for good.

   SEE ALSO
	mailbox.resource/MB_StringCommand(),
	mailbox.resource/MB_ObtainLock()

mailbox.resource/MB_ReleaseLock               mailbox.resource/MB_ReleaseLock

   NAME
	MB_ReleaseLock -- let the other tasks use the mailbox again (V1.2)

   SYNOPSIS
	MB_ReleaseLock()

	VOID MB_ReleaseLock(VOID);

   FUNCTION
	Releases the lock taken by MB_ObtainLock(). The lock nests: it is
	given back to the other tasks when the owner has released it as
	many times as it has obtained it.

   NOTE
	Call it from the task which called MB_ObtainLock(), once for each
	call. Exists from version 1.2 of the resource, see
	MB_ObtainLock().

   SEE ALSO
	mailbox.resource/MB_ObtainLock()

mailbox.resource/MB_SetClockRate             mailbox.resource/MB_SetClockRate

   NAME
	MB_SetClockRate -- set a clock's rate

   SYNOPSIS
	rate = MB_SetClockRate( clock_id, speed )
	d0                      d0        d1

	ULONG MB_SetClockRate(ULONG, ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Sets the rate of a VideoCore-managed clock, using property tag
	SET_CLOCK_RATE (0x00038002).

   INPUTS
	clock_id        The clock to set, by the firmware's id: 1 EMMC, 2 UART,
	                3 ARM, 4 CORE, 5 V3D.
	speed           The desired rate in Hz.

   RESULTS
	rate            The rate actually applied by the firmware, or -1 on a
	                mailbox transport error.

   EXAMPLES
	#include <proto/exec.h>
	#include <proto/dos.h>
	#include <proto/mailbox.h>

	APTR MailboxBase = OpenResource("mailbox.resource");
	if (MailboxBase)
	{
	    ULONG rate = MB_SetClockRate(3, 600000000);          /* 3 = ARM clock */
	    Printf("ARM clock set to: %ld Hz\n", rate);
	}

   NOTE
	As of V1.1 the call gives up after three seconds without an answer from
	the firmware and returns -1. Prior to V1.1 it had no time limit and no
	way to report a failure: a mailbox that did not answer stopped the task
	for good.

   SEE ALSO
	mailbox.resource/MB_GetClockRate()

mailbox.resource/MB_SetClockState           mailbox.resource/MB_SetClockState

   NAME
	MB_SetClockState -- enable or disable a clock

   SYNOPSIS
	state = MB_SetClockState( clock_id, state )
	d0                        d0        d1

	ULONG MB_SetClockState(ULONG, ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Enables or disables a VideoCore-managed clock, using property
	tag SET_CLOCK_STATE (0x00038001).

   INPUTS
	clock_id        The clock to set, by the firmware's id: 1 EMMC, 2 UART,
	                3 ARM, 4 CORE, 5 V3D.
	state           Bit 0: 1 to turn the clock on, 0 to turn it off.

   RESULTS
	state           The state actually applied, or -1 on a mailbox
	                transport error.

   EXAMPLES
	#include <proto/exec.h>
	#include <proto/dos.h>
	#include <proto/mailbox.h>

	APTR MailboxBase = OpenResource("mailbox.resource");
	if (MailboxBase)
	{
	    ULONG state = MB_SetClockState(2, 1);                /* 2 = UART clock */
	    Printf("UART clock state: %ld\n", state);
	}

   NOTE
	As of V1.1 the call gives up after three seconds without an answer from
	the firmware and returns -1. Prior to V1.1 it had no time limit and no
	way to report a failure: a mailbox that did not answer stopped the task
	for good.

   SEE ALSO
	mailbox.resource/MB_GetClockState()

